--TEST--
Unary `+` hands back every numeric type unchanged and is total at the two bounds where `-` throws — ADR 0007 §§ 2, 4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0007 § 4 gives unary `+` no row of its own because there is nothing
// for one to say: over `int`, `uint`, `float` and `decimal` alike it hands
// its operand back. That is asserted here by *agreement* — each column
// renders the operand and its plus-prefixed self and reports whether the
// two match — so a `+` that grew an opinion about one type fails even
// though every line still prints plausibly.
class Plus {
    public static function agrees(string $a, string $b): string {
        if ($a == $b) {
            return "=";
        }
        return "!";
    }
}

int $i = 7;
uint $u = 3;
float $f = 2.5;
decimal $d = 1.25;
echo Plus::agrees($i as string, (+$i) as string),
    Plus::agrees($u as string, (+$u) as string),
    Plus::agrees($f as string, (+$f) as string),
    Plus::agrees($d as string, (+$d) as string),
    "\n";

// It composes with the negation rather than cancelling it, on either side.
echo +(-$i), ",", -(+$i), "\n";

// The bound is where the two operators part. ADR 0007 § 4 makes `-i64::MIN`
// an `ArithmeticError` — there is no `int` to hold it, and the ADR refuses
// to wrap or promote — while `+` over the very same value cannot fail,
// because it is the identity and emits no instruction to fail in.
int $min = -9223372036854775807 - 1;
echo +$min, "\n";
try {
    echo -$min, "\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "throws\n";
}

// `uint`'s own top is the same asymmetry read from the other end: every
// non-zero `uint` negates out of the type, and none of them is unplussable.
uint $top = 18446744073709551615;
echo +$top, "\n";
try {
    echo -$top, "\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "throws\n";
}
--EXPECT--
====
-7,-7
-9223372036854775808
throws
18446744073709551615
throws
